    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • Hybrid Honda Passport Throws More HP in Baja Racing Than Any Other 9.0-Liter V8-Powered Trophy Truck\nAudi Finally Ends A8 Luxury Sedan Production After 32 Years, New Gen Under Consideration\n16 photos\nPhoto: Audi\nAudi's 7 Series and S-Class fighter spent more than three decades making its case for German engineering and luxury in a full-size package. Unfortunately, the A8 has left the Neckarsulm factory that built every generation since its debut.\nThe plant stopped making A8s in October 2026, now focusing on the A5 line of sedans and wagons. Ingolstadt, however, is working out what comes next for its flagship luxobarge. Its canning did not happen overnight, for Audi stopped accepting new orders for the full-size model in February 2026.\nThis left customers to choose from available inventory, which is a big no-no in this particular segment where options and customization are of the essence. As of this writing, the A8 is marketed for the 2027 model year in the US market. Back in March 2026, namely one month after Ingolstadt pulled the plug on it, Audi made it clear that a new generation is under consideration.\nTo be revealed by decade's end, the newcomer could be a very different animal from the last generation. For starters, Audi could very well use the Volkswagen Group architecture of the next-generation Panamera. That model is due to enter series production at Porsche's Leipzig facility around 2030, and sharing underpinnings clearly makes financial sense.\nA different proposal comes from German business publication Manager Magazin, which reports that Audi has considered a luxury sedan for the Chinese market. Huawei, together with either FAW or SAIC, would give Audi established technology and manufacturing relationships.\nPhoto: Audi The People's Republic of China is a very interesting recipient, be it a Volkswagen Group design or something developed in collaboration with local heavyweights. Large and luxurious sedans retain a strong market position there, much stronger than in Western markets, but such a China-focused A8 would make little sense if Audi cannot tailor it according to Chinese preferences.\nWhile Audi may still consider a flagship sedan important enough to develop, its future production arrangements remain under discussion. For the time being, the Q9 sport utility vehicle serves as the brand's series-production flagship. At the other end of the spectrum, the Nuvolari supercar is best described as the German brand's take on the V8-powered Lamborghini Temerario.\nCurrently priced at $95,100 in the US market, the A8 comes with the S line exterior, OLED taillights, and 22-way comfort front seats by default. Its base powertrain is the group's familiar 3.0-liter turbocharged V6, which offers 335 horsepower and plenty of torque from low engine speeds. Due to quattro all-wheel drive, 60 miles per hour (97 kilometers per hour) takes only 5.6 seconds.\nThe much pricier S8 can do a lot better, though. Gifted with a V8, the performance-oriented version packs 563 horsepower and 590 pound-feet (800 Newton-meters) of twist, enough to propel the European luxobarge to 60 in a mere 3.8 seconds. This generation of the A8 is called D5, and can you believe that Audi started making it nine years ago?\nWhatever the future holds for the A8, rival automakers Mercedes and BMW are likely happy that Audi is no longer an option in this particular segment. As it stands, the Sonderklasse and the 7 Series have only the Genesis G90 and the outgoing Lexus LS to worry about.\nVia // Manager Magazin\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nAudi A8 2027 audi a8 Audi production sedan luxury\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT Don’t Miss This Pristine Dodge Challenger Hemi Scat Pack Shaker, It’s a Single-Owner Muscle Car",
